Top DevOps tools every engineer should actually understand 

𝟭. 𝗗𝗼𝗰𝗸𝗲𝗿
→ Packages an app with everything it needs to run, so it behaves the same everywhere
→ Images are built in layers - understanding layers is how you reduce size and speed up builds
→ Most production issues come from networking and volume misconfigurations, not the image itself

𝟮. 𝗞𝘂𝗯𝗲𝗿𝗻𝗲𝘁𝗲𝘀
→ Manages containers at scale - restarting, scheduling, and load balancing automatically
→ The Control Plane decides, the Worker Nodes execute - knowing this split is the foundation of troubleshooting
→ Most real-world issues aren't "Kubernetes is broken" - they're networking, resource limits, or probe misconfigurations

𝟯. 𝗧𝗲𝗿𝗿𝗮𝗳𝗼𝗿𝗺
→ Defines infrastructure as code so environments are reproducible, not manually clicked together
→ The state file is the single source of truth - corrupting it is more dangerous than any resource it manages
→ terraform plan tells you intent, not guarantee - drift outside Terraform can still surprise you

𝟰. 𝗝𝗲𝗻𝗸𝗶𝗻𝘀
→ Automates build, test, and deploy steps so releases aren't manual and error-prone
→ Declarative pipelines are easier to maintain; Scripted gives more control but more complexity
→ A pipeline that's green doesn't mean the deployment worked - verification steps matter as much as the build

𝟱. 𝗔𝗪𝗦 (𝗼𝗿 𝗮𝗻𝘆 𝗰𝗹𝗼𝘂𝗱)
→ Gives you on-demand infrastructure instead of buying and managing physical servers
→ IAM is the most underestimated service - most security incidents trace back to permission misconfigurations
→ Cost and architecture are connected - a "working" setup can still be a bad design if nobody's watching the bill

𝟲. 𝗣𝗿𝗼𝗺𝗲𝘁𝗵𝗲𝘂𝘀 + 𝗚𝗿𝗮𝗳𝗮𝗻𝗮
→ Collects and visualizes metrics so you know about problems before users complain
→ Alerts are only useful if they're tied to something actionable - alert fatigue is a real failure mode
→ A dashboard showing "green" doesn't always mean healthy - it means whatever you chose to measure looks fine

𝟳. 𝗚𝗶𝘁
→ Tracks every change so nothing is ever truly lost, even when it feels like it
→ Branching strategy matters more than most people think - it decides how painful releases are
→ Most "Git disasters" are recoverable - knowing reflog and reset can save you from what feels like a deleted history
